			<div class="content" id="post-content-4841872">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> Let's make conception convenient! Just put in a drop of that prime stock D-N-A and receive a child in under an hour! Perfect for the busy mommies and hard working daddies! The children even come out with little improvements, a gift from our home to yours!</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The Foundation recovers numerous instances of the same anomalous appliance from the 1950s found within an old storage unit. Thought to be an unsuccessful product that never went into circulation, the Foundation begins testing the devices different settings using Class-D personnel. They produce children on every setting the machine has, finding each to be differently cybernetic but otherwise non anomalous. They designate the SCP safe and put it under secure storage at a low security site and debate what to do with the children; while some more paranoid and morally conscious staff members initially recommend euthanasia, the children are healthy despite their implants and found to develop normally. The children are then placed in Foundation care until they can be neutralised if further anomalies develop from them or they can be integrated into the Foundation.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> Initially just an anomalous item, the children produced raise the question of what the Foundation does with the innocent in the aftermath of the anomalous. While the more cynical among us would expect the worst, the Foundation has proven to be surprising and the situation proves both their compassion and their pragmatism.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> I like the idea of a small story and otherwise basic SCP being the catalyst for one of the Foundation's more compassionate moments in history. Sometime I feel they're pushed beyond morally grey pragmatists into villainous territory and think they have the capacity to be kind, even if it's tinged with clinical coldness and only just once in a while.</p>

							</div>

								
					
